# Web Image Format

For every image added to or updated in this project:

- Use **WebP** for all raster images displayed on the website.
- If given PNG, JPG, JPEG, TIFF, BMP, or another raster format, convert it to
 WebP **before** adding it to the website.
- Optimize for the smallest practical size while preserving good visual quality.
- Update all code, imports, paths, and references to point at the `.webp` file.
- Preserve transparency when the source has it (`sharp` keeps the alpha channel;
 do not flatten onto a background).
- Keep **SVG** as SVG for logos, icons, diagrams, and other vector graphics.
- Do **not** convert animated GIFs automatically — ask first whether they should
 become animated WebP or a video.
- Do not keep the original raster file unless it is explicitly needed. Delete it
 and remove stale references.
- Before finishing, verify the converted image actually loads in the browser and
 report its final path.

## Converting

```bash
node scripts/to-webp.mjs <src> <dest> [quality]   # quality defaults to 82
```

Quality 82 has been landing around 65% smaller than the PNG source. `sharp` is
resolved from the pnpm store rather than being a direct dependency, so no
install step is needed.

Always check the real dimensions of the source before wiring it up — a file's
extension can lie (an image named `.png` may actually be a JPEG), and any
`aspect` value in `site.config.ts` must match the true pixel dimensions or the
image renders distorted.

## Two deliberate exceptions

These stay PNG. They are not "displayed on the website", and converting them
breaks real behavior:

- `public/og-image.png` — the Open Graph / Twitter share card. Several social
 crawlers still will not render WebP, so converting silently breaks link
 previews everywhere.
- `public/assets/brand/logos/png/**` and `public/assets/brand/logos/cards/*.png`
 — downloadable brand-kit assets that press and partners expect as PNG.
 Generated by `scripts/generate-brand-pngs.mjs`.

## Verifying

After converting, load every affected page and confirm no image has
`naturalWidth === 0` and no request returns 4xx. Scroll to the bottom first so
lazy-loaded images actually fetch.
